## Variable Naming

### Be descriptive — avoid abbreviations

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const [d, setD] = useState([]);
const usr = await fetchUser();
function calc(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
items.forEach((e, i) => console.log(i, e));

// ✅ Good
const [clients, setClients] = useState<Client[]>([]);
const user = await fetchUser();
function calculateTotal(subtotal: number, taxRate: number) {
  return subtotal * (1 + taxRate);
}
items.forEach((item, index) => console.log(index, item));
```

### Naming patterns

| Context            | Pattern                    | Example                                       |
| ------------------ | -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Boolean state      | `is/has/should` prefix     | `isLoading`, `hasError`, `shouldRefetch`      |
| Event handlers     | `handle` prefix            | `handleClick`, `handleSubmit`, `handleChange` |
| Callbacks as props | `on` prefix                | `onClick`, `onSubmit`, `onChange`             |
| Arrays             | Plural nouns               | `users`, `items`, `notifications`             |
| Counts             | Noun + `Count`             | `userCount`, `itemCount`                      |
| Maps/Records       | Noun + `Map` or `By` + Key | `userMap`, `usersById`                        |

### Accepted short names

These are acceptable due to widespread convention:

- `id` — identifier
- `url` — URL
- `api` — API
- `db` — database (in backend contexts)
- `x, y, z` — coordinates
- `i, j, k` — loop indices (only in simple numerical loops)

