## Project Structure

We use a **feature-first architecture** for frontend and **domain-based organization** for Convex backend:

```
project-root/
├── app/                           # Expo Router (screens only)
│   ├── (auth)/                    # Auth flow
│   ├── (protected)/               # Authenticated routes
│   └── users/                     # Dynamic routes
│       └── [id].tsx
│
├── convex/                        # Backend (domain-based)
│   ├── _generated/
│   ├── schema.ts
│   ├── http.ts
│   │
│   ├── users/                     # User domain
│   │   ├── queries.ts            # All user queries
│   │   ├── mutations.ts          # All user mutations
│   │   ├── internal.ts           # Internal-only functions
│   │   └── helpers.ts            # Shared helpers (not exported)
│   │
│   └── messages/                  # Message domain
│       ├── queries.ts
│       ├── mutations.ts
│       └── helpers.ts
│
├── features/                      # Frontend features (vertical slices)
│   └── {feature}/
│       ├── components/           # Feature components
│       ├── hooks/                # Convex wrappers + logic
│       ├── stores/               # UI state only (NOT server data)
│       ├── types/                # TypeScript types
│       └── index.ts              # Public exports
│
├── components/                    # Shared components
│   ├── ui/                       # UI primitives (RN Reusables)
│   ├── layout/                   # Layout components
│   └── shared/                   # Shared business components
│
├── hooks/                         # Shared hooks
├── stores/                        # Global Zustand stores
├── lib/                           # Utilities
├── types/                         # Shared types
└── providers/                     # Context providers
```

### Import rules

```typescript
// ✅ Import from feature's public API
import { UserCard, useUserList } from '@/features/users';

// ✅ Import Convex functions
import { api } from '@/convex/_generated/api';
import { useQuery, useMutation } from 'convex/react';

// ✅ Import UI components
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
import { ScreenWrapper } from '@/components/layout/screen-wrapper';

// ❌ Never import internal modules
import { UserCardInternal } from '@/features/users/components/internal/user-card';

// ❌ Never import Convex helpers directly
import { getCurrentUser } from '@/convex/users/helpers';
```

### Feature Index Export Patterns

**Decision Rule: Count files in each subfolder**

```
Algorithm:
1. For each subfolder (hooks/, components/, types/, stores/)
2. Count number of files
3. IF any subfolder has >= 10 files:
     USE Pattern B (Subfolder Indexes)
   ELSE:
     USE Pattern A (Explicit Exports)
```

#### Pattern A: Explicit Exports (Small Features)

Use when **all subfolders have <10 files**.

```typescript
// features/users/index.ts
// Subfolder file counts: hooks=2, components=0, types=1, stores=0
// Max count = 2 < 10, so use explicit exports

export { useCurrentUser } from './hooks/use-current-user';
export { useUpdateDisplayName } from './hooks/use-update-display-name';
export type { User } from './types/user.types';
```

**Advantages:**
- ✅ Clear public API at a glance
- ✅ Better IDE "Go to Definition" (direct to file)
- ✅ Easier code review
- ✅ Better tree-shaking

**Use this pattern when:**
- Feature is small to medium
- All subfolders have <10 files
- You want explicit control over public API

#### Pattern B: Subfolder Indexes (Large Features)

Use when **any subfolder has 10+ files**.

```typescript
// features/projects/hooks/index.ts
// hooks/ has 15 files, so add subfolder index
export { useProjects } from './use-projects';
export { useProject } from './use-project';
export { useCreateProject } from './use-create-project';
export { useUpdateProject } from './use-update-project';
export { useDeleteProject } from './use-delete-project';
export { useProjectMembers } from './use-project-members';
export { useProjectSettings } from './use-project-settings';
export { useProjectTasks } from './use-project-tasks';
export { useProjectFiles } from './use-project-files';
export { useProjectActivity } from './use-project-activity';
export { useProjectAnalytics } from './use-project-analytics';
export { useProjectExport } from './use-project-export';
export { useProjectImport } from './use-project-import';
export { useProjectShare } from './use-project-share';
export { useProjectArchive } from './use-project-archive';

// features/projects/components/index.ts
// components/ has 12 files
export { ProjectCard } from './project-card';
export { ProjectList } from './project-list';
// ... 10 more exports

// features/projects/index.ts (root)
// Just re-export from subfolder indexes
export * from './hooks';
export * from './components';
export type * from './types';
```

**Advantages:**
- ✅ Less maintenance for large features
- ✅ Easy to add new files (just update subfolder index)
- ✅ Organized by domain

**Use this pattern when:**
- ANY subfolder reaches 10+ files
- Feature is large/complex
- Public API changes frequently

#### Anti-Pattern: Premature Subfolder Indexes

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Unnecessary indirection for 2 files
// features/users/hooks/index.ts (only 2 hooks!)
export * from './use-current-user';
export * from './use-update-display-name';

// features/users/index.ts
export * from './hooks';  // Extra hop for IDE navigation

// ✅ GOOD: Direct exports for small features
// features/users/index.ts
export { useCurrentUser } from './hooks/use-current-user';
export { useUpdateDisplayName } from './hooks/use-update-display-name';
```

**Problems with premature subfolder indexes:**
- Adds unnecessary files
- Slower IDE navigation (extra hop)
- Harder to see full public API
- No benefit for small features
- Complexity without gain

