## TypeScript Guidelines

### Use Convex auto-generated types

Convex automatically generates types from your schema. **Never manually duplicate them.**

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Use Convex's Doc<> type
import { Doc } from '@/convex/_generated/dataModel';

export type User = Doc<'users'>;

// ❌ WRONG - Manual duplication (gets out of sync)
export type User = {
  _id: Id<'users'>;
  _creationTime: number;
  clerkId: string;
  displayName: string;
  email?: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
};
```

**Why use Doc<>:**
- Single source of truth (schema is the only definition)
- Auto-updates when schema changes
- Includes system fields (`_id`, `_creationTime`)
- Full TypeScript safety from database to client

### Prefer `interface` for object shapes

```typescript
// ✅ For object shapes (extendable)
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

// ✅ For union types
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive' | 'pending';

// ✅ For function types
type FormatFn = (value: number) => string;
```

### Use `import type` for type-only imports

```typescript
// ✅ Good
import type { User, Client } from './types';
import type { FC, ReactNode } from 'react';

// ❌ Avoid mixing runtime and type imports
import { User, formatUser } from './types';
```

### Avoid `any`

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
function process(data: any) { ... }

// ✅ Good
function process(data: unknown) { ... }
function process<T>(data: T) { ... }
function process(data: Record<string, unknown>) { ... }
```

### Const assertions for literal types

```typescript
// ✅ Good
const STATUS = {
  ACTIVE: 'active',
  INACTIVE: 'inactive',
} as const;

type Status = (typeof STATUS)[keyof typeof STATUS];
// Result: 'active' | 'inactive'
```

